diagnosis_text:
A full, layered diagnostic sweep of the simple_bgp lab finds no fault of any kind. In Phase 1, bidirectional reachability between pc1 (195.11.14.2) and pc2 (200.1.1.2) is confirmed at 0 % packet loss with sub-2 ms round-trip times. Phase A (L2 / infrastructure) shows all four devices with clean addressing, no ACL or firewall drop rules, no static-ARP poisoning, no duplicate MAC addresses, and every interface in the UP/REACHABLE state. Phase B (control plane) shows both FRR stacks fully operational — watchfrr, zebra, and bgpd are running on both router1 and router2, the BGP session between them is Established, each router correctly advertises its local prefix (195.11.14.0/24 from router1, 200.1.1.0/24 from router2), and there are no null-route or blackhole entries. Traffic shaping is absent (all qdiscs are stock fq_codel). Phase D (service / resource pressure) confirms no stress processes, no CPU spikes, and no abnormal socket counts. The link-history check finds zero carrier-change anomalies or DHCP-recovery events on any device, ruling out a past or intermittent flap. The helpdesk report of a downed inter-building link is not corroborated by any current evidence or historical trace in the live lab. Root cause: no anomaly is present — the network is fully healthy and the user-reported outage cannot be reproduced or traced to any device, interface, or protocol fault in the current lab state.
